  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report on Power Marketing Administrations (PMAs)**\n\nThe Power Marketing Administrations (PMAs) are four regional agencies operated by the Department of Energy to market and sell electric power produced by federal dams. The PMAs are: Bonneville Power Administration (BPA), Southeastern Power Administration (SEPA), Southwestern Power Administration (SWPA), and Western Area Power Administration (WAPA). Each PMA operates in a distinct geographic area of the United States and has unique elements and regional issues that affect its business.\n\nThe PMAs were created to dispose of excess electric power produced by federal dams and to provide service to small communities and farms that would not be profitable for private utilities to serve. The PMAs must give preference to public utility districts and cooperatives, selling power at cost-based rates set at the lowest possible rate consistent with sound business principles.\n\nThe report highlights the financial health and challenges faced by each PMA. BPA, the largest PMA, is self-financed and has a debt of $5.53 billion, which it repays through electricity sales. SEPA, the smallest PMA, has an overcapacity issue and has had to terminate contracts with some customers. SWPA has been challenged by low water conditions, which require it to purchase replacement power and energy to meet its contractual obligations. WAPA, the newest and largest PMA, has been working to address the changing electric power needs of its customers and has implemented a drought-adder reduction program to help smooth annual differences in power generation.\n\nThe report also discusses recent proposals to sell the transmission assets owned and operated by the federal PMAs. The Trump Administration proposed to sell the transmission assets, which would have resulted in a net budgetary savings of $9.5 billion over a 10-year window. However, the proposal was dropped due to stakeholder opposition.\n\nThe report concludes that the PMAs play a critical role in providing electricity at the lowest possible cost to customers, and that any changes to their structure or authority would require careful consideration of the potential impacts on their ability to meet this mandate.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n* The PMAs are critical to providing electricity at the lowest possible cost to customers.\n* Each PMA has unique elements and regional issues that affect its business.\n* The PMAs must give preference to public utility districts and cooperatives, selling power at cost-based rates.\n* The PMAs face financial challenges, including debt and overcapacity issues.\n* Recent proposals to sell the transmission assets owned and operated by the federal PM",
